::PHY-U1-L7-Q01::Pressure equals {=force divided by area ~force times area ~mass divided by volume ~work divided by time}
::PHY-U1-L7-Q02::The SI unit of pressure is {=pascal ~newton ~joule ~watt}
::PHY-U1-L7-Q03::The same force on a smaller area produces {=greater pressure ~less pressure ~zero pressure ~unchanged pressure}
::PHY-U1-L7-Q04::Fluid pressure generally {=increases with depth ~decreases with depth ~is independent of density ~is always zero}
::PHY-U1-L7-Q05::In P=rho gh, rho represents {=fluid density ~depth ~gravity ~pressure}
::PHY-U1-L7-Q06::Compression tends to {=push material together ~pull material apart ~slide material only ~remove all stress}
::PHY-U1-L7-Q07::Tension tends to {=pull material apart ~push material together ~eliminate strain ~increase density only}
::PHY-U1-L7-Q08::Shear involves forces that {=act parallel in opposite directions ~act equally inward ~act equally outward ~produce no deformation}
::PHY-U1-L7-Q09::Stress is best described as {=force per area within a material ~mass per volume ~energy per time ~distance per time}
::PHY-U1-L7-Q10::Strain describes {=deformation caused by stress ~force per area ~fluid pressure only ~mass change}
::PHY-U1-L7-Q11::Mechanical weathering {=breaks rock physically without changing composition ~moves sediment only ~melts rock ~always changes minerals chemically}
::PHY-U1-L7-Q12::Erosion is the {=transport of weathered material ~formation of pressure ~increase of density ~creation of mass}
::PHY-U1-L7-Q13::Topography describes {=surface shape and elevation ~rock age only ~air pressure only ~ocean salinity}
::PHY-U1-L7-Q14::A positive relationship between crustal thickness and elevation means {=thicker crust tends to correspond to higher elevation ~thicker crust always lies below sea level ~elevation causes mass to vanish ~no pattern exists}
::PHY-U1-L7-Q15::Isostasy is most closely related to {=gravitational and buoyant equilibrium of the crust ~projectile motion ~electric current ~nuclear decay}
::PHY-U1-L7-Q16::A mountain root extends into the mantle mainly because {=thicker low-density crust displaces mantle material ~gravity stops acting ~erosion adds mass ~pressure is zero}
::PHY-U1-L7-Q17::Removing mass by erosion can lead to {=isostatic uplift ~permanent loss of buoyancy ~zero gravity ~greater fluid density}
::PHY-U1-L7-Q18::Mountain building commonly involves {=compressive forces and crustal thickening ~only tension and thinning ~no external forces ~constant zero stress}
::PHY-U1-L7-Q19::Mountain height is limited partly because greater height produces {=greater stress at depth ~less weight ~zero erosion ~weaker gravity}
::PHY-U1-L7-Q20::Rock begins to deform or fail when stress {=exceeds its strength ~equals zero ~removes all strain ~is measured in meters}
::PHY-U1-L7-Q21::A 200 N force acts on 0.50 m^2. Pressure is {=400 Pa ~100 Pa ~200 Pa ~0.0025 Pa}
::PHY-U1-L7-Q22::Doubling fluid depth while density and g stay constant makes gauge pressure {=double ~half ~unchanged ~zero}
::PHY-U1-L7-Q23::At the same depth, a denser fluid produces {=greater pressure ~less pressure ~the same pressure always ~no force}
::PHY-U1-L7-Q24::A useful mountain model should include {=forces, material properties, and a stated limitation ~only a decorative drawing ~no labels ~a claim without evidence}
::PHY-U1-L7-Q25::Which statement best connects uplift and erosion? {=They can occur simultaneously and together shape elevation ~They are identical processes ~Neither involves force or energy ~Erosion always stops uplift immediately}

